South Sudan, the world’s youngest nation, has faced persistent challenges since its independence in 2011. Despite enduring ethnic conflict, political instability, and humanitarian crises, it has received significantly less international media attention compared to other regions in crisis, such as Ukraine, Palestine, and Israel. This disparity in media representation influences global perceptions, policy responses, and aid allocations. Using data-driven methods, this research examines how South Sudan is represented in international media, compares this representation to coverage of Ukraine, Palestine, and Israel, and explores the role of vested interests in shaping coverage. By employing computational techniques, including topic modeling, keyword analysis, and contextual analysis, the study provides actionable recommendations for improving the visibility of South Sudan in global narratives that will ultimately help South Sudan.
